{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nSplit out of `d26a-8ffa-97cd-4b2a` (`dire-expectable-terrapin`), where these were carried as\nits DEFERRED AC5 and AC6. That bug fixed the key-path DIVERGENCE that made the deployed MCP\nserver sign op-certs it could never verify (Gerrit 2337, merged as\n`83edf9972728ae1e0729a39098ec70e4fab4617a`): verification now resolves the public half from the\nsame ordered chain the signing side resolves the private half.\n\nWhat that fix did NOT add is any GUARD. `sign_manifest` still mints a certificate without\nchecking that the environment can resolve a public counterpart for the key it just signed\nunder, and nothing at deploy/startup surfaces it when it cannot. So the CLASS of defect — a\nsilently uncertifiable attestation — can regress the moment a new deployment shape puts the\nsigning key somewhere the resolution chain does not reach. Signing succeeds; only a later\n`verify_signature` (or a refused claim) reveals the problem, potentially long after the\ncertificates were minted.\n\n## Approach\nTwo independent, additive guards, neither of which changes an existing success path:\n\n1. At MINT: after resolving the signing key, ask the verify-side resolver\n   (`_opcert_own_public_keys`, `src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py`) whether a public counterpart is\n   resolvable for it. If not, emit an explicit WARNING naming the resolved key path and the\n   principal. It must NOT refuse — refusing would break every existing sign flow and is a\n   behaviour change beyond this bug's scope.\n2. At STARTUP/DEPLOY: the MCP server's existing health surface (`src/rebar/_mcp_health.py`)\n   reports a DEGRADED field and logs a boot WARNING when the bound startup signer's public key\n   is not the pinned trusted-environment key for its principal — the ONE same-environment\n   failure the (derivative) public key cannot otherwise catch. SERVE-DEGRADED, never abort\n   boot (operator ruling; ADR 0104 decision 3 makes required-environment binding advisory\n   today), mirroring `store_status` / `run_startup_store_sweep`.\n\nBoth are in-process checks driven by an operation and by server startup, with no dependency on\nany particular CI provider.\n\n### AC3 reframing (operator ruling, recorded here)\nThe op-cert public key is DERIVATIVE (`_derive_opcert_pub` = `ssh-keygen -y`, re-derivable on\ndemand from the private key), and `compose_startup_opcert_binding` already fails closed on a\nbad/missing/encrypted/non-Ed25519 private key. So a \"can we resolve/derive a public cert?\"\nstartup probe would be REDUNDANT. The one genuinely non-redundant condition is whether the\nbound signer's public key MATCHES the environment's pinned/published trusted-environment key\n(`rebar.attest.trusted_env`) — the key required-environment verify checks against. Even that is\nnon-blocking: those are still valid signed attestations that only fail an advisory\nenvironment-binding check, so AC3 emits a degraded health field + a warning and does NOT refuse\nto serve or abort boot.\n\n## Scope\n- In scope: the mint-time warning and the startup/health serve-degraded surface.\n- Out of scope: the key-resolution chain itself (already fixed by 2337); any change to the\n  cross-environment `foreign_key` verdict, which is documented intended behaviour (ADR 0104),\n  not a defect; extending `compose_startup_opcert_binding` to abort; a redundant derive probe.\n\n## Testing\nUnit tests: a mint under a key whose public half cannot be resolved emits the warning and still\nreturns a signature; a mint under a resolvable key emits no warning; the startup/health check\nreports degraded (and warns) for a bound signer whose public key is not the pinned key, and is\nquiet/non-degraded for a matched key, for an unbound signer, and for a deployment that has not\nopted into pinning; key matching is on the key type+base64 body (comment-insensitive); a\nresolution fault is surfaced as degraded (not swallowed); a `RemovedInputError` fails hard.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] AC1: `sign_manifest` emits an explicit WARNING — naming the resolved key path and the principal — when the environment cannot resolve a public counterpart for the key it just signed under, and still returns the signature (no refusal, no existing sign flow broken).\n- [ ] AC2: `sign_manifest` emits no such warning when the public counterpart IS resolvable.\n- [ ] AC3: the MCP server's startup/health surface reports a DEGRADED `opcert` field and logs a boot WARNING when the bound signer's public key is not the pinned trusted-environment key for its principal, and is non-degraded/quiet when it matches (or when unbound / pinning not configured). Non-blocking: it NEVER aborts boot (serve-degraded; ADR 0104 decision 3).\n- [ ] AC4: both guards are in-process and operation-linked, with no dependency on a specific CI provider.\n\n## Definition of Done\n- [ ] DD1: AC1 and AC2 covered by a test that is RED before the change and GREEN after it.\n- [ ] DD2: AC3 covered by a test over the startup/health check (degraded, matched, unbound, and not-pinned cases)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787973560065320001,"uuid":"25cee18c-a6ac-4031-a037-ee69e55b7a23"}